          <p>
            <strong>Quick rule:</strong> if you would consider a behavior disrespectful when
            meeting someone face-to-face, it is usually worth reconsidering online as well.
          </p>

          <h2>3. Cultural Sensitivity: Understanding Before Judging</h2>

          <p>
            Every culture has its own communication norms — gestures, phrases, or even silences
            that carry different meanings. A thumbs-up might be a friendly sign in one country but
            rude in another. That’s why cultural sensitivity is at the heart of proper online
            etiquette.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you notice something unfamiliar, don’t jump to conclusions. Instead, ask politely.
            For example, “I noticed you use this hand gesture often — does it have a specific
            meaning in your country?” This not only shows interest but also helps avoid
            misinterpretations. If you want to know what I learned after
            <a href="/blog/what-i-learned-talking-to-people-from-20-different-countries">
              talking to people
            </a>,
            read the full story.
          </p>

          <div
            class="blog-research-box"
            style="width:100%;max-width:900px;margin:24px auto;padding:20px;box-sizing:border-box;border:1px solid #5a76a3;border-radius:12px;background:#263245b3;"
          >
            <h3 style="margin-top:0;">
              Research Insight: Nonverbal Signals Can Be Misread
            </h3>

            <p style="margin-bottom:12px;">
              In video conversations, people communicate through more than words. Facial
              expressions, gestures, pauses, eye contact, and other visual signals can influence
              how a message is interpreted.
            </p>

            <p style="margin-bottom:0;">
              Research on intercultural communication shows that people from different cultural
              backgrounds may interpret the same nonverbal behavior differently. This is why asking
              for clarification is often safer than assuming that a gesture or expression has the
              meaning you expect.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            I once chatted with someone from Japan who would slightly bow before leaving the call.
            At first, I thought it was a glitch in the camera! Later, I learned it was a cultural
            sign of gratitude and respect. Ever since, I’ve started ending calls with a small hand
            wave and a smile — small gestures that cross borders without language.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pro tip:</strong> Never assume. Curiosity connects; assumptions divide.
          </p>

          <h2>4. Stay Safe While You’re Being Social</h2>

          <p>
            Kindness doesn’t mean letting your guard down. While making international friends is
            rewarding, safety should always come first. Avoid sharing personal details like your
            phone number, home address, or daily schedule. Keep conversations friendly and fun,
            but avoid giving strangers too much information about your private life.
          </p>

          <p>
            The website includes AI-assisted and human moderation to help reduce harmful behavior.
            It automatically detects inappropriate behavior and helps maintain respectful
            conversations. If anyone crosses a line or makes you uncomfortable, use the
            <em>Report</em> or <em>Skip</em> options immediately. Remember, your safety comes
            before politeness.
          </p>

          <p>
            Online friendships thrive on trust, not on exchanging personal data. True friends will
            respect your boundaries and enjoy conversations without needing your contact info.
          </p>

          <h3>What Information Is Safe to Share Early?</h3>

          <p>
            Being friendly does not require revealing private information. A useful rule is to
            start with information that helps conversation while avoiding details that could
            identify, locate, or financially affect you.
          </p>

