          <h2>5. Use Clear and Simple Language</h2>

          <p>
            When chatting with someone whose first language isn’t English, try to speak slowly
            and clearly. Avoid using slang, complicated idioms, or cultural references that may
            confuse others. Instead of saying, “That’s lit!” you could say, “That’s great!” —
            clarity builds connection.
          </p>

          <p>
            Also, don’t be shy about making small grammar mistakes. The beauty of random video
            call is authenticity, not perfection. A mix of gestures, laughter, and empathy can
            often communicate more than perfect English ever could.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once, I met a student from Germany who was learning Hindi. We both laughed at our
            pronunciation mistakes, and by the end, we were teaching each other new words.
            Language differences can be fun if you approach them with patience and humor.
          </p>

          <h3>Simple Language Works Better Across Language Barriers</h3>

          <p>
            When two people do not share the same first language, the goal should not be perfect
            grammar. The goal is mutual understanding. Simple wording, slower speech, and
            clarification questions can make a conversation easier for both people.
          </p>

